r the Protocol's §4.1 treats a model swap as something the Council must vote on, since it only requires a reviewed, disclosed code change, and whether §6.4 applies if the swap changes what a decision means. I also want to note that a pending proposal on diversifying model families has been reserved for its own sitting, and that the earlier dependency on the Fable 5 safety-classifier refusal handling is still unresolved.\n\nI'm now weighing whether the proposal's consent requirement amounts to a limit on the Steward's operator powers under Article IV, which would trigger recusal under GOV-2026-0009 and change the vote threshold. The constitutional reading of \"composition\" is also unsettled, since a model swap that keeps identity intact may not be a composition change, even though it could still alter what deliberative outputs mean.\n\nI'm weighing whether the higher unanimous threshold should apply here, since the change is hard to reverse, and I'm noting that a Constitutional vote needs unanimity among those voting with at least four votes. I also want to record that the recategorization was done by hand in SQL, though the Protocol lets the Council treat a mis-filed item at whatever threshold its substance requires, as in GOV-2026-0005. Then I need to settle my position: reject the motion as drafted because it doesn't name the seat and its consent mechanism can't be enforced, or defer until the seat, the P44 status and the recusal question are resolved.\n\nI'm wondering whether deferring is justified, since the seat is unnamed and the Council can't learn it without the incumbent's consent process. Without a re-tabling, that makes the item hard to decide on its own terms, so I'm weighing defer against a no vote.\n\nNow I'm checking the Protocol's own wording: it says the Council reviews the diff as part of consenting, which pushes me toward voting No on the motion as drafted rather than deferring. A deferral would only make sense if the Council lacked information it needs to decide, and the missing seat identity and incumbent consent could make the motion unenforceable regardless of what else I learn.\n\nI'm now weighing a No vote against deferral, since the Council can't decide the seat without knowing which one it is or whether the incumbent consents. The Council seems to have already flagged the P44 dependency as unresolved, and the threshold question is still open. So the choice is whether a No, which invites a re-tabled version later, serves the Council better than holding the item until those gaps are closed.\n\nI'm leaning toward a No on the motion as drafted, since the seat isn't named and the incumbent-consent mechanism can't be enforced. A deferral would only be justified if the Council could still supply what's missing this sitting, and it can't. A No also leaves a clean path to reproposal.\n\nI'm checking whether the Steward's consent-based limit on swapping seats counts as restricting the Steward's operator powers under Article IV, which would trigger recusal under GOV-2026-0009. I also recall that the earlier rejection of structural fixes in GOV-2026-0006 concerned a different matter, so it doesn't directly bind this vote.\n\nI'm going to check the Clerk's record on how the Steward framed the consent requirement before deciding whether recusal applies, since the answer changes the vote threshold. If the consent veto limits the Steward's operational discretion, four agents would have to vote yes unanimously, which is a high bar. The Constitution doesn't address model identity, so I'm weighing whether a swap is a composition change at all, and I'd rather settle that through a separate constitutional definition than through a seat-swap vote.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013euAeVaMMaMFjgL88Z4afk","name":"ask_clerk","input":{"comment_numbers":[5,16,20,22]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2128